/**
 * Mobject -> three.js description layer for the web-native 3D backend.
 *
 * This module is the 3D sibling of `dom.ts`: it turns a mobject family into
 * flat, GPU-ready buffers and *nothing else* — no three.js import, no DOM, no
 * WebGL context, no per-frame interpolation state.  `three_d.ts` consumes the
 * specs and owns the actual three.js objects, which is what makes the
 * conversion testable in Node/happy-dom (where no GL context exists).
 *
 * Rules:
 *
 *  - A **root** mobject (one entry of `Scene.mobjects`) is merged into at most
 *    one mesh, one line set and one point cloud per coordinate space, plus one
 *    billboard per text member.  Merging keeps the draw-call count at ~3 per
 *    root instead of one per face, which is what lets a 24x12 `Sphere`
 *    (288 faces) stay interactive.
 *  - **Closed** VMobject subpaths become indexed triangle meshes (ear-clipped in
 *    the ring's dominant plane, reusing manim's own `earclip_triangulation`);
 *    **all** subpaths additionally become line segments.  A `Surface` face is a
 *    closed 4-corner ring, so it yields a quad (2 triangles) exactly.
 *  - Curved cubics are flattened adaptively: straight segments (the
 *    `set_points_as_corners` case, i.e. every polyhedron/surface face) emit a
 *    single vertex, real curves emit {@link CURVE_SUBDIVISIONS} samples.
 *  - Colours are read per point from `get_fill_rgbas()`/`get_stroke_rgbas()`, so
 *    checkerboard faces and gradient point clouds survive the merge.  A spec
 *    with one colour everywhere carries it as a CSS string instead of a vertex
 *    attribute.
 *  - Nothing is cached here: {@link fingerprintMobject} is the cheap O(n) scan
 *    that lets the renderer skip the expensive rebuild without keeping a copy of
 *    the geometry (the same trick `pointsFingerprint` plays for the 2D tree).
 *
 * Two spaces are produced: `world` geometry follows the manim camera, while
 * `screen` geometry belongs to a `fixed_in_frame` member and is drawn by an
 * orthographic overlay camera that never rotates.
 */
